WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:06.330
Ok so gadolinium is smack-bang in the middle
of the 4f series. It’s

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.740
perhaps most famous for the fact that it’s
used as a contrast reagent in

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.389
MRI machines. So if you need a full body scan,
a doctor will come along

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:19.861
an inject you with a solution which contains
a bit of gadolinium and what

00:00:19.861 --> 00:00:23.940
it basically does is that it changes the way
that molecules, water

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.770
molecules react in your body when they are
scanned and then you can

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:32.750
see the contrast between healthy tissue and,
perhaps, unhealthy tissue

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.149
and then that aids the doctors in their assignment.

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:40.680
The thing about the lanthanide series is that
it’s probably the best

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.330
example of a smooth contraction in size as
you move from the left-hand

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:51.610
side to the right-hand side and you lose about
25% of the radius of the

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.750
atoms as you move from the left to the right
and this is called the

00:00:54.750 --> 00:01:00.300
‘lanthanide contraction.’ This is actually
what’s responsible for 2nd and 3rd

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.299
row transition metals having very similar
types of chemistry by group

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:10.109
even though actually they should be quite
different. The 3rd row should

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:13.759
be much larger but, because of the ‘lanthanide
contraction’, that

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:17.520
increase in size is counterbalanced so they
actually end up being more-

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.409
or-less the same size as their 2nd row counterparts.

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:25.689
Even chemists sometimes wonder how people
can get excited about

00:01:25.689 --> 00:01:31.770
particular parts of chemistry, and the rare
earth elements are elements

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.959
which, those who are not working in the area
don’t always see why it can

00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:43.539
be very exciting. So when I was a student
I saw an advertisement in the

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:49.429
lab where I was working about a lecture about
the oxidation states of the

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:55.549
rare earth elements, and the relative stability
of two of the oxidation

00:01:55.549 --> 00:02:00.590
states by a chemist called David Johnson,
who in those days worked for

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:07.299
the Open University. And, I went to this lecture
because I couldn’t

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:10.860
imagine how anybody could make it interesting,
and it was really

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:16.760
fascinating! He was so enthusiastic and so
on, by the end, I rushed out

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.580
really quite excited I went and bought this
book which has been sitting

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.450
on my bookshelf ever since and has been quite
useful for teaching some

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.140
of my students.

